5G HETEROGENEOUS NETWORK 
This document provides a visual and textual overview of 5G heterogeneous 
network architecture, which uses multiple layers of cell types to 
optimize coverage, capacity, and speed.

Multi‑layer nonlinear system:

           Macro/micro = LTI stable modes
           Small/mmWave = nonlinear, hybrid, mode‑switching dynamics
           Blockage = state jumps
           Beam tracking = feedback control
           Handover = hybrid transition
           Mobility = time‑varying A(t)
           Rain fade = perturbation
           RIS/massive MIMO = feedback stabilization (A − BK)
           Throughput/SINR = output y(t)
           Network optimization = LQR/H∞‑like control problem

System State

           x(t) — Network state at time t  

Includes: user position, beam direction, link quality, LOS/NLOS 
condition, cell tier (macro/micro/small/femto), blockage state.

Control Input

           u(t) — Network control actions

Beam steering, power allocation, handover decisions, scheduling, 
modulation/coding selection.

Output

           y(t) — User‑perceived performance

Throughput, latency, SINR, coverage quality.

Dynamics
Because mmWave propagation is nonlinear, blockage‑driven, and 
environment‑dependent:

           ẋ(t) = f(x(t), u(t)) 

Nonlinear dynamics describing mobility, attenuation, beam tracking, 
and cell transitions.

Sub‑6 GHz macro/micro layers behave more like LTI approximations:

           ẋ(t) = Ax + Bu  

Where A encodes path‑loss and mobility, B encodes power/beam control.

