📡 LOS vs NLOS Path Loss — Quantitative Comparison (mm Wave)
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Takeaway: At mmWave, NLOS links are typically 20–40 dB worse than LOS at 
the same distance, and sometimes >50 dB worse depending on materials and 
geometry.

LOS (Line‑of‑Sight) Path Loss
Dominated by free‑space loss
Scales with frequency² (Friis)
Predictable and stable

Typical values at 28 GHz:
100 m: ~93–96 dB
200 m: ~99–102 dB
300 m: ~103–106 dB

NLOS (Non‑Line‑of‑Sight) Path Loss
Dominated by reflection, scattering, and diffraction loss

Highly dependent on environment

Typical values at 28 GHz:
100 m: ~120–140 dB
200 m: ~130–150+ dB
300 m: ~140–160+ dB

LOS vs NLOS Delta (28 GHz)
Distance	LOS Path Loss     NLOS Path Loss	 Δ (Extra NLOS Loss)
100 m	93–96 dB	       120–140 dB	 +25–45 dB
200 m	99–102 dB	       130–150+ dB	 +30–50 dB
300 m	103–106 dB	       140–160+ dB	 +35–55 dB

LOS vs NLOS
 LOS path loss ≈ stable LTI mode
 NLOS path loss ≈ nonlinear jump mode

Blockage causes:
           state discontinuity
           mode switching
           large Δx(t)

Rain Fade
 Acts like a time‑varying A(t):
  heavy rain → A(t) becomes more negative → faster decay of signal.

